<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="512" height="512" viewBox="0 0 512 512" fill="currentColor">
<!-- TWO RIFLE ROUNDS, the mark for a HUMAN COMBAT (bounty / assault) task, drawn inside
     the task reticle rather than in a badge ring. Game-icons.net "bullets" (CC BY 3.0),
     the same set the mutant skull came from.

     IT REPLACED CROSSED SWORDS (R2.58), kept at svg/bounty-swords.alt, which in turn had
     replaced a bust. Read bounty-swords.alt before touching this: the constraint it was
     chosen under still governs this cell and has not changed.

     THE CONSTRAINT, restated because it is the only thing that matters here. This mark's
     red (172,60,66) and the mutant hunt's lime (176,216,72) sit on the red-green axis,
     which is the axis red-green colour deficiency collapses. For those players SHAPE IS
     THE ONLY CHANNEL separating "a firefight" from "a mutant hunt". Any candidate has to
     be told apart from svg/skull.svg AT 26 PX WITH THE COLOUR REMOVED. Do not reason
     about the tints; render both cells to luminance and look.

     THIS CLEARS THAT BAR, and the swords note's own prediction about it was wrong. That
     note rejected "paired rounds" on the grounds that they "read as two bars rather than
     as ammunition", which is true and turns out not to be the objection it sounds like:
     two bars is a DIFFERENT SILHOUETTE CLASS from a round centred mass, which is the
     whole test. Reading as ammunition was never the requirement. Rendered to luminance at
     26 px the pair is unmistakable against the skull, and it beats the swords there - an X
     of thin crossing blades is the one thing the 8x downsample does not survive, and the
     swords smudge into a dark blob at exactly the size the map draws them at.

     ASPECT IS WHY IT WORKS WHERE A SINGLE PISTOL DID NOT. fit() sizes the LONG axis, so a
     landscape glyph gets a haircut on the short one: the pistol was 1.53:1 and collapsed
     to a bar, this pair is 1.30:1 and does not. It is also all thick solid strokes, which
     is the property that survives the downsample (see svg/home.svg for the same argument).

     RETICLE_FIT_SCALE stays at 1.15 and this is a ceiling, not a spare knob. At 1.30 the
     ink comes up to the swords' but the tips break through the reticle's arcs in the ring
     style and through the frame in the s2 diamond - measured and looked at, both fail.

     THE SOURCE DRAWS THREE ROUNDS, as one path of six subpaths: a casing and a tip per
     round. This keeps the first two rounds by taking the first four subpaths. Trim FROM
     THE END ONLY - subpath 4 (the second round's tip) is a RELATIVE moveto, so it cannot
     be lifted out without the casing before it, and dropping an earlier subpath silently
     moves every relative one after it. -->
<path d="m162.322 17.943-28.316 105.682 18.053 4.838 4.194-15.654 169.764 45.487 11.28-42.088-169.872-45.105 4.838-18.052 169.87 45.103 4.038-15.066L176.404 37.6l3.97-14.82-18.052-4.837zM361.336 98.7l-14.453 53.943c53.99 13.97 102.986 20.59 150.95 10.964-37.1-32.648-82.453-50.034-136.497-64.906zM128.676 145l-41.82 101.104 17.27 7.142 6.216-15.023L272.75 305.4l16.36-39.556-162.43-67.12 7.142-17.27 162.432 67.118 6.254-15.12-162.406-67.18 5.843-14.13-17.27-7.142zm186.818 105.95-21.344 51.605c51.717 20.872 99.435 33.81 148.24 30.502-32.538-37.196-75.244-60.332-126.896-82.106z" transform="translate(0,0)" />
</svg>
